<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/cryptography">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    Three earlier lessons built the ground you stand on here. <em>Networking: how the internet
    works</em> (advanced) showed you packet switching — this lesson opens by treating every
    packet as readable, alterable and injectable by an adversary. <em>Cybersecurity: threats and
    defences</em> (advanced) catalogued the attacks; here you meet the mathematical machinery
    that answers them. And <em>Computability and complexity</em> (the sibling expert lesson)
    supplied the hardness assumptions the whole subject rests on: crypto is only as strong as
    the problems it asks attackers to fail at.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>separate the three jobs of a secure channel — confidentiality, integrity, authentication — and name the primitive that does each;</li>
    <li>explain why error-detection codes catch noise but not adversaries, and what a MAC adds;</li>
    <li>trace the Diffie–Hellman exchange and say which hard problem shields it;</li>
    <li>justify the hybrid design that pairs RSA-style key agreement with AES bulk encryption;</li>
    <li>read the padlock icon as a chain of signatures, and name its operational failure modes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cryptography is <strong>mathematics as a trust machine</strong>: making secrets provably hard
    to read, signatures impossible to forge, and tampering detectable — without trusting the
    channel, the carrier, or even the other party's honesty. Modern crypto rests on
    <em>hardness assumptions</em>: problems (factoring, discrete logs) believed intractable. When
    the assumptions shift (quantum computers), the crypto must too. But the full subject is
    bigger than encryption alone: before you can protect data you must know how it travels, and
    before you can trust what arrives you must separate <em>accidental</em> corruption (which
    error-detection codes handle) from <em>deliberate</em> tampering (which only cryptographic
    authentication handles). That three-layer picture — transmission, integrity, secrecy — is the
    spine of this chapter, and of the padlock icon you check without thinking.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five ideas between you and the padlock icon" widget="equationStep" config={cryptoCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panels tell the story in dependency order. Panel 1 ends
    on a problem (how do two strangers share a key?), panel 2 answers it, and panel 3 generalises
    the answer into public locks. Panels 4 and 5 then add what encryption alone never provided —
    tampering evidence and identity. When you reach the last panel, note that none of the five
    steps is "encryption makes data safe": the padlock is assembled, not switched on.
  </p>

  <h2>Types and methods of data transmission: what the attacker sees</h2>
  <p>
    Data moves in characteristic patterns, and each exposes a different attack surface.
    <strong>Direction</strong>: simplex (one way only — a broadcast sensor), half-duplex (both
    ways, one at a time — walkie-talkie), full-duplex (both at once — your phone call). <strong>Mode
    of the link</strong>: serial sends bits one after another down a single wire (slow per pin but
    reliable over distance — every modern bus: USB, SATA, Ethernet); parallel sends a byte across
    many wires at once (fast over centimetres, hopeless over metres because skew makes bits
    arrive out of step). <strong>Timing</strong>: asynchronous transmission frames each byte with
    start and stop bits (keyboards, serial consoles) while synchronous transmission locks sender
    and receiver to a shared clock and moves data in large blocks (networks, disks). Internet
    transmission is <strong>packet-switched</strong>: messages are sliced into packets that route
    independently, queue in buffers, and can arrive reordered, duplicated or not at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why this matters to the cryptographer: on a packet-switched public network you must assume an
    adversary can <em>read</em> any packet (confidentiality problem), <em>alter</em> packets in
    flight (integrity problem), and <em>inject or replay</em> new ones (authentication problem).
    The man-in-the-middle café network is not a hypothetical — it is the default assumption, and
    the entire protocol stack below exists because of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the adversary model is set — read, alter, inject. The next layer
    handles the cheapest threat to reason about, accidental corruption, and its limits are
    exactly what force the cryptographic machinery that follows.
  </p>

  <h2>Error detection: parity, checksums and CRC</h2>
  <p>
    Cosmic rays, cable noise and buffer bugs corrupt data constantly, so honest systems add
    redundancy in layers. <strong>Parity</strong> appends one bit so the number of 1s is even
    (even parity): it catches any single-bit flip but misses any even number of them — a 2-bit
    error passes undetected half the time, which is why parity guards memory buses (where 1-bit
    flips dominate) but not networks. <strong>Checksums</strong> sum the data and send the total:
    better, but reordering two words, or adding and subtracting equal errors, slips through.
    <strong>Cyclic Redundancy Checks</strong> treat the message as a polynomial and divide it by a
    fixed generator polynomial, sending the remainder; the choice of polynomial makes CRC-32 catch
    <em>all</em> burst errors of 32 bits or fewer and all odd numbers of bit flips — the reason
    Ethernet frames, ZIP files and every storage device use it. The <strong>Hamming
    distance</strong> is the theory underneath: a code that keeps every two valid codewords at
    least d apart can detect up to d − 1 flipped bits and correct up to ⌊(d − 1)/2⌋ — the
    arithmetic behind RAID 6 (two parity blocks: any two failed disks survivable) and deep-space
    communication (Voyager still talks to Earth on Reed–Solomon codes).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "an error-detecting code also detects tampering." It detects
    <em>noise</em>, not adversaries — the CRC of a modified packet is recomputable by anyone, so a
    man-in-the-middle edits the message <em>and</em> its check field in one step. Protection
    against intentional alteration requires a keyed construction (a MAC: a hash seeded with a
    secret key), which is a cryptographic primitive, not a transmission code. Confusing the two
    has shipped broken protocols.
  </p>

  <h2>Encryption, part one: symmetric ciphers and the key problem</h2>
  <p>
    Symmetric encryption uses one secret key for both directions, and the modern standard is
    <strong>AES</strong> (Rijndael, standardized 2001): a substitution–permutation network that
    runs the 128-bit block through 10, 12 or 14 rounds of byte substitution (S-box), row shifts,
    column mixing and round-key addition. Its security budget is the keyspace: AES-128 has
    2<sup>128</sup> ≈ 3.4 × 10<sup>38</sup> keys; at a fantasy trillion guesses per second,
    exhausting it takes about 10<sup>19</sup> years — roughly a billion times the age of the
    universe. Brute force is not the threat; implementation errors, key reuse (never reuse a
    nonce in counter mode) and key <em>distribution</em> are. Distribution is the ancient flaw:
    symmetric encryption is nearly useless between strangers, because the key must already have
    travelled by some secure path — the problem that stymied every pre-1976 system from Caesar to
    Enigma, and the problem the next section dissolves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> symmetric ciphers give you overwhelming strength with one fatal
    dependency: the key must already be shared. Hold that unsolved problem in mind as you read
    the next section — it is the single most consequential idea in the history of the subject.
  </p>

  <h2>Encryption, part two: public keys and the key-exchange miracle</h2>
  <p>
    Diffie and Hellman (1976) showed two strangers broadcasting nothing secret can still agree on
    a secret. Each picks a private number a and b, publishes g<sup>a</sup> mod p and
    g<sup>b</sup> mod p, and each computes (g<sup>b</sup>)<sup>a</sup> = (g<sup>a</sup>)<sup>b</sup>
    = g<sup>ab</sup> mod p — the shared key — while the eavesdropper, seeing g, g<sup>a</sup> and
    g<sup>b</sup>, faces the <em>discrete logarithm problem</em>: recovering a from g<sup>a</sup>
    is computationally hopeless for large p (with 2,048-bit primes, beyond any known classical
    machine). <strong>RSA</strong> (1978) turned the same one-way idea into encryption and
    signatures: publish a modulus n (a 617-digit product of two primes) and an exponent; anyone
    can encrypt to you, and only the holder of the private prime factors can decrypt. Real
    systems are <strong>hybrid</strong>: asymmetric crypto exchanges a session key (once, for one
    connection), then AES encrypts the traffic (fast, bulk) — that handshake is the padlock
    appearing in your browser, and forward secrecy (ephemeral keys per session) means even a
    later server compromise cannot decrypt yesterday's capture.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "asymmetric is stronger, so why keep AES at all?" The two are
    not competitors. RSA at 2,048 bits offers roughly 112-bit security and costs about a
    thousandth of AES's speed; encrypting gigabytes with it would be ruinous. The hybrid design
    uses each tool where it wins — public-key for key agreement and signatures, symmetric for
    data — which is exactly what TLS, Signal and every VPN do.
  </p>

  <h2>Hashes, signatures and the chain of trust</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>cryptographic hash</strong> (SHA-256) maps any input to a 256-bit fingerprint with
    three properties: one-way (no inverse), avalanche (change one bit and about half the output
    bits flip), collision-resistant (no practical way to find two inputs with the same hash).
    Hashes alone give integrity checking for downloads; combined with a private key they become a
    <strong>digital signature</strong>: sign the hash, and anyone with your public key verifies
    both origin and integrity in one operation. The last link is <strong>identity</strong>: a
    certificate binds a public key to a name (your bank's), signed by a certificate authority
    that your operating system and browser pre-trust, whose own keys are signed by roots in the
    trust store. The padlock icon is that chain verified — and its failure modes are
    operational, not mathematical: authorities have been compromised (DigiNotar, 2011, issued
    fake Google certificates and was dissolved), which is why certificate transparency logs now
    make every issued certificate publicly auditable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "encryption makes data safe, full stop." Encryption protects
    one state of the data — on the wire (TLS) or on disk (at rest). It does nothing about the
    endpoints (your browser and the server still see plaintext), nothing about stolen passwords,
    misconfigured databases or insiders, and nothing once an employee pastes the secret into a
    chat. Threat modelling starts where the cipher ends.
  </p>

  <p>
    Key management is where real systems actually fail, and the modern answer to its
    sharpest question — "what happens when today's key leaks tomorrow?" — is
    <strong>forward secrecy</strong>. Ephemeral Diffie–Hellman (the "E" in TLS's DHE/ECDHE)
    generates a fresh key for every session and erases it: steal the server's long-term
    private key later and you can impersonate the server, but you still cannot decrypt
    last week's recorded traffic, because the session keys no longer exist anywhere. This
    is why Signal's double ratchet re-derives keys per message, why WhatsApp's 2021
    backdoor debate hinged on metadata rather than message content, and why "rotate your
    keys" is a design property, not a policy slogan.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sophisticated attacker often skips the maths entirely and attacks the
    <strong>implementation</strong>. Side channels leak secrets through physical
    behaviour rather than mathematical structure: Paul Kocher's 1996 timing attack
    recovered RSA keys purely from how long decryption operations took, and the
    2003 remote-timing results (Boneh and Brumley) showed the same was possible over
    a network. The defence — constant-time code, blinding, identical execution paths
    regardless of secret bits — is why crypto libraries read like they are wasting
    cycles on purpose. A formally correct cipher running in non-constant time is not
    a secure system; the guarantee lives at every layer or not at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does a CRC catch every burst error up to 32 bits yet fail completely against a deliberate attacker?</li>
    <li>Walk through Diffie–Hellman: Alice publishes g<sup>a</sup> mod p, Bob publishes g<sup>b</sup> mod p. What does each compute, what does the eavesdropper see, and which problem protects the secret?</li>
    <li>TLS encrypts gigabytes of traffic with AES, not RSA. Why does the hybrid design win, and which numbers from this lesson quantify the answer?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The generator polynomial is chosen so all burst errors of 32
    bits or fewer leave a nonzero remainder — but the CRC is unkeyed: anyone who alters the
    message recomputes the valid remainder in one step. Detecting noise is arithmetic; detecting
    adversaries requires a keyed construction (a MAC). (2) Alice computes (g<sup>b</sup>)<sup>a</sup>,
    Bob computes (g<sup>a</sup>)<sup>b</sup> — both equal g<sup>ab</sup> mod p, the shared key.
    The eavesdropper sees only g, g<sup>a</sup> and g<sup>b</sup>, and recovering a from
    g<sup>a</sup> is the discrete logarithm problem — hopeless for large p on any known classical
    machine. (3) RSA at 2,048 bits offers roughly 112-bit security at about a thousandth of
    AES's speed, so bulk encryption with it would be ruinous; AES has the keyspace budget but no
    way to distribute keys between strangers. The hybrid uses public-key crypto once per session
    to agree a session key, then AES for the traffic — which is the handshake behind the padlock.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Packet-switched networks demand an adversary model of read, alter and inject — every layer of the protocol stack answers one of those.</li>
    <li>Error-detection codes (parity, checksums, CRC) catch noise; only keyed constructions catch adversaries.</li>
    <li>AES's 2<sup>128</sup> keyspace makes brute force hopeless; the real risks are implementation errors, nonce reuse and key distribution.</li>
    <li>Diffie–Hellman lets strangers derive a shared secret in public, shielded by the discrete logarithm problem; RSA turns one-way maths into encryption and signatures.</li>
    <li>Hybrid design assigns each tool where it wins: public-key for agreement and signatures, symmetric for bulk data.</li>
    <li>Hashes, signatures and certificates assemble the padlock — a chain of trust ending in roots you agreed to trust, auditable via certificate transparency.</li>
    <li>Forward secrecy and constant-time implementations guard against the failures that survive perfect mathematics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Two sibling expert lessons extend this one: <em>Distributed systems</em> applies the same
    primitives to consensus among machines that share no perimeter, and <em>Compilers and
    languages</em> shows how type systems and memory safety eliminate the bug classes that
    implementation attacks feed on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>What "provably secure" means.</strong> Shannon's one-time pad (key as long as the
    message, used once) is the only cipher with <em>perfect secrecy</em> — ciphertext leaks
    literally zero information — and its impracticality motivates the modern standard instead:
    reductions. A scheme is proven secure if breaking it would solve a stated hard problem
    (factoring, discrete log); the guarantee is conditional but precise, and the whole discipline
    of provable security lives in making those assumptions and games explicit. Kerckhoffs's
    principle (1883) remains the foundation: assume the enemy knows the system; secrecy must live
    in the key alone — which is why "security through obscurity" is a recognised anti-pattern and
    open algorithms win.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The quantum deadline.</strong> Shor's algorithm (1994) factors integers and computes
    discrete logs in polynomial time on a large quantum computer — collapsing RSA and
    Diffie–Hellman wholesale. No machine today can factor a 2,048-bit number, but "harvest now,
    decrypt later" changes the calculus for long-lived secrets: traffic captured today can be
    decrypted in 2035. NIST standardised the first post-quantum algorithms in 2024 — ML-KEM
    (from CRYSTALS-Kyber) for key exchange and ML-DSA for signatures, built on lattice problems
    with no known quantum attack — and Chrome, Signal and major browsers already negotiate
    hybrid classical-plus-PQ handshakes. The migration is the rare engineering project whose
    deadline is defined by an adversary's hardware roadmap.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where crypto touches daily engineering.</strong> Password storage is the most common
    real deployment: never store passwords (even hashed with SHA-256 — billions of guesses per
    second on GPUs), store <em>deliberately slow, salted</em> hashes (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2),
    which cost attackers real time per guess. Message encryption in Signal's double ratchet
    rotates keys per message so a compromised key decrypts only one message forward. And the
    blockchain lesson's hashes are the same primitives: SHA-256 chaining blocks, signatures
    authorising spends — the trust machine applied to money. The expert habit across all of them:
    use audited standard constructions, and treat the urge to invent your own as the first bug.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Diffie & Hellman (1976); Rivest, Shamir & Adleman (1978); NIST Post-Quantum Cryptography standards (2024); Kerckhoffs (1883). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
